1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,680
Tessa's mortgage fallback costs $120 a
month.

2
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:10,800
She's borrowing $320,000, and in her city
that payment is her biggest bill.

3
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:16,200
A 15-year mortgage, or a 30-year paid off
in 15 years anyway,

4
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:19,320
with its lower payment kept for a bad
month?

5
00:00:19,933 --> 00:00:26,120
We replayed every week of US mortgage
surveys to price that fallback in dollars,

6
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:29,080
and what each extra dollar of principal
earns.

7
00:00:30,067 --> 00:00:33,427
Tessa is illustrative, but her rates are
real:

8
00:00:33,447 --> 00:00:40,307
the survey for the week of October eighth,
2026 has the 15-year at 6.73 percent and

9
00:00:40,327 --> 00:00:42,867
the 30-year at 7.4 percent.

10
00:00:43,043 --> 00:00:47,107
This is US only, and it's history, not a
forecast.

11
00:00:47,347 --> 00:00:50,947
It won't say which loan to take, or
whether to invest.

12
00:00:54,933 --> 00:00:56,373
Here is what Tessa is weighing.

13
00:00:56,693 --> 00:01:03,693
On $320,000, the 15-year loan's payment is
$2,828 a month.

14
00:01:05,213 --> 00:01:11,733
The 30-year loan only requires $2,216.

15
00:01:12,733 --> 00:01:17,533
That gap, about $613 a month, is the
fallback:

16
00:01:17,586 --> 00:01:21,293
in a tight month, it's the least the
30-year asks of her.

17
00:01:21,453 --> 00:01:25,293
The bigger payment fits her budget today,
but only just,

18
00:01:25,313 --> 00:01:28,493
and a job change or a big repair could
squeeze it.

19
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:33,373
Still, she wants the house paid off in 15
years,

20
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:38,253
so the 30-year only works for her if she
pays it like a short loan.

21
00:01:39,267 --> 00:01:46,267
At 7.4 percent, finishing that loan in 15
years takes $2,948 a month.

22
00:01:48,531 --> 00:01:54,147
That's $120 a month more than the 15-year
loan,

23
00:01:54,167 --> 00:01:57,747
for the same house and the same 15 years.

24
00:01:57,843 --> 00:02:04,843
Over those 15 years, the extra adds up to
$21,622.

25
00:02:06,233 --> 00:02:10,713
In Tessa's world, that price is a stack of
bills beside her house:

26
00:02:10,733 --> 00:02:13,273
the stack is what keeping the fallback
costs.

27
00:02:13,486 --> 00:02:18,953
It exists because the 30-year carries a
higher rate on the same balance:

28
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:22,953
on October eighth, 0.67 point higher.

29
00:02:23,967 --> 00:02:30,967
Paid on its own 30-year schedule, her loan
would charge $477,622 in interest.

30
00:02:33,167 --> 00:02:40,047
Paid in 15 years, it charges $210,691.

31
00:02:41,033 --> 00:02:48,033
The 15-year loan charges $189,069, and the
difference between those last two totals

32
00:02:48,713 --> 00:02:50,713
is exactly the stack.

33
00:02:51,060 --> 00:02:57,113
So the 30-year paid in 15 isn't the same
loan as the 15-year:

34
00:02:57,166 --> 00:03:02,473
it's the 15-year plus a fallback, and the
fallback has a price.

35
00:03:02,953 --> 00:03:07,113
Is Tessa's stack a normal price for that
fallback,

36
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:10,553
or is the week of October eighth unusual?

37
00:03:12,067 --> 00:03:17,107
Many walk-throughs of this question use
one assumed rate and treat paying early as

38
00:03:17,127 --> 00:03:18,387
the obvious win.

39
00:03:18,627 --> 00:03:24,787
Here, a real 15-year loan stands next to
the 30-year paid in 15,

40
00:03:24,947 --> 00:03:29,027
at the real rates of every week since
1991.

41
00:03:29,347 --> 00:03:36,347
We priced the same $320,000 both ways in
each of 1,833 weeks,

42
00:03:37,934 --> 00:03:43,907
at that week's own survey rates; how it
was built is on screen and in the

43
00:03:43,954 --> 00:03:44,954
description.

44
00:03:45,307 --> 00:03:51,587
Tessa's week is the last of them, and in
every earlier week we asked her question:

45
00:03:51,779 --> 00:03:54,787
what would the fallback have cost then?

46
00:03:55,800 --> 00:04:01,160
The answer comes down to one thing: how
far the 30-year rate sits above the

47
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:02,480
15-year.

48
00:04:02,553 --> 00:04:09,000
Since 1991, that gap has run from 0.2
point to 1.0 point.

49
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:16,600
It averaged 0.57 point, and was at least
half a point in about 2 in 3 weeks.

50
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,320
Tessa's gap on October eighth sits a
little above that average.

51
00:04:22,333 --> 00:04:27,613
Stand a stack beside every week, and the
stacks rise and fall with that gap.

52
00:04:27,633 --> 00:04:30,493
The smallest was $6,097.

53
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:35,500
The tallest was $29,502.

54
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:42,580
The typical week, the median, priced the
fallback at $16,631.

55
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:48,180
Fewer than 1 in 5 weeks since 1991 put a
price this high on it.

56
00:04:48,356 --> 00:04:51,540
That makes Tessa's fallback expensive by
history.

57
00:04:51,684 --> 00:04:54,500
What a lower payment is worth in a bad
month,

58
00:04:54,548 --> 00:04:56,180
this replay can't measure.

59
00:04:57,167 --> 00:05:01,327
Line the weeks up by decade, and the
pattern is not the one most people expect.

60
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:07,247
In the 1990s, the typical fallback cost
$15,118.

61
00:05:08,233 --> 00:05:12,473
In the 2000s, with lower rates, it cost
about the same:

62
00:05:12,517 --> 00:05:15,593
$14,210.

63
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:21,400
In the 2010s, rates were far lower, yet
the fallback cost more:

64
00:05:21,446 --> 00:05:24,360
$20,374.

65
00:05:25,367 --> 00:05:32,367
The reason is the gap: in the 2010s and
2020s it typically ran about 0.7 point,

66
00:05:32,867 --> 00:05:37,607
against about 0.45 in the 1990s and 2000s.

67
00:05:37,967 --> 00:05:41,447
A lower rate didn't mean a cheaper
fallback;

68
00:05:41,487 --> 00:05:44,167
a wider gap meant a pricier one.

69
00:05:45,167 --> 00:05:51,247
Since 2020, the typical week priced it at
$21,345,

70
00:05:51,314 --> 00:05:53,247
close to Tessa's own stack.

71
00:05:53,471 --> 00:05:58,207
That's history, not a forecast: the gap
has moved before,

72
00:05:58,247 --> 00:06:00,607
and nothing here says where it goes.

73
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:05,920
Back to Tessa's own loan, and a shortcut
many people reach for:

74
00:06:05,980 --> 00:06:09,600
pay the 30-year exactly what the 15-year
would cost,

75
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:11,760
and assume it ends on the same day.

76
00:06:11,973 --> 00:06:17,200
At the October eighth rates, it doesn't:
it ends about 15 months late.

77
00:06:17,387 --> 00:06:24,320
To finish in 15 years, she'd add $733 a
month on top of the required payment.

78
00:06:25,333 --> 00:06:28,453
That extra never lowers what the loan
requires:

79
00:06:28,473 --> 00:06:32,773
the required payment stays where it was,
and the loan simply ends sooner.

80
00:06:32,965 --> 00:06:36,453
Each extra dollar does cut the next
month's interest;

81
00:06:36,473 --> 00:06:40,293
the payment just stays fixed, so more of
it goes to principal.

82
00:06:40,405 --> 00:06:43,093
That's why the fallback is still there
years later,

83
00:06:43,146 --> 00:06:44,373
if a month goes wrong.

84
00:06:45,367 --> 00:06:49,687
Tessa has another practical question:
could she send the extra once a year

85
00:06:49,727 --> 00:06:50,807
instead of every month?

86
00:06:50,935 --> 00:06:54,327
That's one payment a year, the same total
as the monthly extra.

87
00:06:54,471 --> 00:06:57,367
Paid that way, the loan ends four months
later.

88
00:06:58,367 --> 00:07:02,767
And it charges about $8,200 more in
interest,

89
00:07:02,797 --> 00:07:06,687
because each dollar sits on the balance
longer before it arrives.

90
00:07:06,879 --> 00:07:11,887
That isn't a verdict on paying yearly: the
money stays in her account longer,

91
00:07:11,907 --> 00:07:14,487
and what it earns there isn't counted.

92
00:07:14,587 --> 00:07:17,567
Which leaves the question underneath all
of this:

93
00:07:17,679 --> 00:07:20,607
when Tessa puts an extra dollar into the
mortgage,

94
00:07:20,719 --> 00:07:22,687
what does it actually earn?

95
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:28,440
Every extra dollar of principal stops
interest from being charged on that

96
00:07:28,497 --> 00:07:31,000
dollar, at the loan's own rate.

97
00:07:31,480 --> 00:07:35,720
On Tessa's 30-year, that's 7.4 percent a
year,

98
00:07:35,766 --> 00:07:36,840
before taxes.

99
00:07:37,107 --> 00:07:42,200
It isn't an investment return; it's
interest she no longer pays.

100
00:07:42,424 --> 00:07:47,240
That rate is the bar any other use of that
dollar gets measured against,

101
00:07:47,297 --> 00:07:48,520
before taxes.

102
00:07:48,740 --> 00:07:53,080
How any investment compares, once risk and
taxes count,

103
00:07:53,140 --> 00:07:54,280
isn't tested here.

104
00:07:55,267 --> 00:07:57,827
The bar moves with the rate a loan was
taken at.

105
00:07:57,934 --> 00:08:03,027
Since 1991, it has stood as high as 9.25
percent.

106
00:08:04,033 --> 00:08:06,993
And as low as 2.65 percent.

107
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:11,760
Only about 1 week in 5 had a bar as high
as Tessa's.

108
00:08:12,767 --> 00:08:18,607
Leo, also illustrative, took a 30-year
loan of the same size in January 2021,

109
00:08:18,647 --> 00:08:22,447
the week of that lowest rate, and paid it
like a 15-year too.

110
00:08:22,647 --> 00:08:29,487
His fallback was cheaper: $13,231 over 15
years.

111
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:37,140
And it left him more room: his required
payment sat about $793 a month below the

112
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:38,200
15-year's.

113
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:45,340
But his bar was 2.65 percent: each extra
dollar he paid earned only that,

114
00:08:45,723 --> 00:08:47,220
before taxes.

115
00:08:47,940 --> 00:08:52,260
Same plan, a different week, and it means
something else.

116
00:08:52,460 --> 00:08:57,300
For Leo, the stack was short and the bar
was low;

117
00:08:57,340 --> 00:09:01,860
for Tessa, the stack is tall and the bar
is high.

118
00:09:02,867 --> 00:09:06,387
Any fixed-rate borrower can set their own
pair beside hers.

119
00:09:06,527 --> 00:09:10,787
The bar is that loan's own rate, printed
on the paperwork,

120
00:09:10,807 --> 00:09:16,747
and the stack depends on how far apart the
15-year and 30-year offers sit that week.

121
00:09:16,857 --> 00:09:22,467
The bar isn't the whole story: a dollar
paid into the house is hard to get back in

122
00:09:22,507 --> 00:09:26,147
a bad month, which is exactly what the
fallback is for.

123
00:09:27,134 --> 00:09:30,813
What this leaves out is on the method card
and in the description.

124
00:09:30,942 --> 00:09:34,654
This is US only, and it's history, not a
forecast;

125
00:09:34,681 --> 00:09:36,413
it doesn't say which loan to take.

126
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:41,400
Beside Tessa's house now stand a tall
stack and a high bar.

127
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:46,520
She asked: a 15-year, or a 30-year paid in
15?

128
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:53,880
What she now knows: the 30-year paid in 15
is the 15-year plus a

129
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:58,520
fallback, and at the rates of the week of
October eighth,

130
00:09:58,554 --> 00:10:05,240
2026, that fallback costs her $120 a
month.

